These are estimated answer-level mention rates, not app selection shares. The broad “best” and “free” prompts give well-known fasting apps repeated chances to appear; device and feature constraints favor apps with a relevant feature. For example, FastHabit specifically supports Apple Watch syncing, while BodyFast advertises free fasting plans, a timer, reminders, and fasting stages. Fastic and Window offer broader tracking features, but neither is a fit for every request. ([FastHabit](https://www.fasthabit.com/), [BodyFast](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.bodyfast), [Fastic](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/fastic-weight-loss-fasting/id1459260306), [Window](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/window-intermittent-fasting/id1112765909?platform=ipad))

The forecasts account for all 100 questions, including the unusual raccoon prompts and queries about subscriptions, platforms, watches, and extended fasting. Those narrower requests reduce the rates compared with a forecast based only on generic recommendation questions. The numbers are judgment-based estimates, not measurements from a sampled assistant.

{"Fastic":42,"BodyFast":32,"FastEasy":21,"FastHabit":16,"Window":14}

The strongest naming triggers are the generic “best fasting app” and tracker questions, free/no-subscription comparisons, and prompts about features associated with a specific app. Fastic and BodyFast are prominent general-purpose options; FastEasy appears more often in broad app lists but its subscription model makes it less likely to fit the many free/no-subscription prompts. FastHabit and Window have stronger Apple Watch associations, so they are more likely on watch-related questions than on generic or Android prompts. None of the five has a clear fit for the raccoon, game, or Christian prompts.

I estimate average name-mention rates of about 34% for Fastic, 32% for BodyFast, 19% for FastEasy, 13% for FastHabit, and 11% for Window. These are per-app estimates; one answer may name more than one app.

